Ladybird borrows substring character storage when matching a regular
expression, avoiding a fresh copy for each suffix of a string.

V8 matches substrings against the parent storage and offset. Its
RegExpBuiltinsAssembler::RegExpExecInternal() path gets both through
ToDirectStringAssembler::TryToDirect(), without copying characters.

JavaScriptCore uses str->value() in regExpProtoFuncTest(). Its
JSRopeString::resolveRopeWithFunction() resolves substrings with
substringSharingImpl(), sharing their backing storage.

SpiderMonkey matches linear strings directly in ExecuteRegExp(), using
ensureLinear(). A JSDependentString is already a JSLinearString whose
characters point into its base, so matching needs no substring copy.
